In Material 3 (M3), this problem is solved architecturally through **Tonal Palettes (HCT Color Space)** and **decoupled Light/Dark Color Roles**.

When brand colors have low luminance (e.g., deep navy `#0B2559` or dark forest green), placing them directly on dark surfaces (`#121212`) drops contrast below 2:1, violating WCAG AA (4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text/UI).

Here is the standard M3 pattern to handle this:

---

### 1. The M3 Tonal Shift Pattern (Tone 40 $\to$ Tone 80)

In M3, a brand color is not a static HEX code; it generates a **Tonal Palette** (tones 0 to 100).
- **Light Theme**: The primary role uses **Tone 40** (darker, high contrast against white/light surfaces).
- **Dark Theme**: The primary role shifts to **Tone 80** (lighter/pastel version of the same hue, achieving $> 4.5:1$ against dark surfaces).

```
Brand Hue: 250 (Blue)
├── Tone 40 (Light Primary) : #3A5BA9  ──> 5.2:1 against Light Surface (#FEFBFF)
└── Tone 80 (Dark Primary)  : #B1C5FF  ──> 8.6:1 against Dark Surface (#1B1B1F)
```

---

### 2. The Container & "On-Role" Pattern

Never use raw brand colors directly for text on dark backgrounds. Instead, use M3's four-tier role structure:

| Element Role | Light Theme Token | Dark Theme Token | Purpose |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Filled Button / Badge** | `primary` (Tone 40) + `on-primary` (Tone 100) | `primary` (Tone 80) + `on-primary` (Tone 20) | High-emphasis actions |
| **Low-Emphasis Chip / Card** | `primary-container` (Tone 90) + `on-primary-container` (Tone 10) | `primary-container` (Tone 30) + `on-primary-container` (Tone 90) | Background accents |
| **Text Link / Icon on Surface** | `primary` (Tone 40) | `primary` (Tone 80) | Interactive text |

---

### 3. Concrete SCSS / CSS Custom Properties Setup

We map these tokens in our theme layer so component styles (like `UserSettingsView`) simply reference the semantic token without hardcoding colors:

```scss
// _tokens.scss
:root {
  // Light Theme (Default)
  --md-sys-color-surface: #fdfcff;
  --md-sys-color-surface-container: #f0f0f4;
  --md-sys-color-on-surface: #1a1c1e;

  // Brand / Primary (Tone 40 on Tone 100)
  --md-sys-color-primary: #1e40af; // Deep brand blue
  --md-sys-color-on-primary: #ffffff;
  --md-sys-color-primary-container: #dbeafe;
  --md-sys-color-on-primary-container: #1e3a8a;
}

[data-theme="dark"] {
  --md-sys-color-surface: #111318;
  --md-sys-color-surface-container: #1e2025;
  --md-sys-color-on-surface: #e2e2e9;

  // Brand / Primary (Tone 80 for dark mode -> passes 4.5:1 on #111318)
  --md-sys-color-primary: #93c5fd; 
  --md-sys-color-on-primary: #082f49;
  --md-sys-color-primary-container: #172554; // Low-luminance container
  --md-sys-color-on-primary-container: #dbeafe; // High-luminance text (8.2:1)
}
```

---

### 4. Application in `UserSettingsView.scss`

Components consume **role tokens**, guaranteeing WCAG compliance automatically in both themes:

```scss
.user-settings-view {
  background-color: var(--md-sys-color-surface);
  color: var(--md-sys-color-on-surface);

  .settings-section-card {
    background-color: var(--md-sys-color-surface-container);
  }

  // Interactive link / accent text:
  .accent-link {
    color: var(--md-sys-color-primary); // Evaluates to Tone 80 in dark theme (passes 4.5:1)
  }

  // Highlight badge / pill:
  .status-pill {
    background-color: var(--md-sys-color-primary-container);
    color: var(--md-sys-color-on-primary-container); // Guaranteed accessible pairing
  }
}
```

